<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alaric II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/507">507</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alaric II was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> from 484 to 507, ruling much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. In 486 he handed over the fugitive Roman ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syagrius">Syagrius</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>, and in 506 he issued the Breviary of Alaric, a digest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> for his Roman subjects. He was killed by Clovis at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vouille">Battle of Vouillé</a> in 507.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaric-ii.jpg" alt="Alaric II" width="320" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anneau_sigillaire_Alaric_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alaric II succeeded his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a> as king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> on 28 December 484. He was the great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaric-i">Alaric I</a>, who sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 410. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> included most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Gallia Aquitania</a> and the greater part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>. His reign is poorly documented: the historian Herwig Wolfram notes that it gets no full treatment in the sources and is overshadowed by his death at Vouillé, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a> gives it a single paragraph, mostly about that battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The earliest recorded event concerns <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syagrius">Syagrius</a>, former ruler of the Domain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, who had been defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and took refuge with Alaric. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, Clovis intimidated Alaric into surrendering him, and Clovis later had him executed. In 490 Alaric sent an army to raise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a>'s siege of Pavia, where his fellow Gothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> was trapped. About 495 Visigothic rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> proved not deeply established, and Alaric took part in repressing the uprising of Burdunellus, which ended at Caesaraugusta in 496. By 502 he and Clovis had met near Amboise and made a peace treaty.</p><p>Alaric was an Arian, like the early Visigothic nobles, but he eased his father's persecution of Catholics and permitted them to hold the council of Agde in 506. He exiled Caesarius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> for a year on suspicion of conspiring with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>. He also appointed a commission under the referendary Anianus to prepare an abstract of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman laws</a> and imperial decrees as the authoritative code for his Roman subjects, known as the Breviary of Alaric.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> broke the peace of 502 and invaded Visigothic territory, despite the diplomatic intervention of Theodoric, Alaric's father-in-law. Forced by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> to fight, Alaric met him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vouille">Battle of Vouillé</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 507. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> were defeated and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, Clovis himself killed Alaric. Clovis took the royal treasury at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, though with Ostrogothic help much of the Gallic territory was later recovered.</p><p>The gravest result was disarray in the kingdom. Alaric left an illegitimate elder son, Gesalec, and a legitimate younger son, Amalaric, still a child. Gesalec proved incompetent, and in 511 Theodoric took the throne ostensibly on Amalaric's behalf. The Montagne d'Alaric near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> is named after him, and local tradition says he buried a vast treasure there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alaric II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 507 he defeated and killed the Visigothic king <b>Alaric II</b> at the Battle of Vouillé, adding most of Aquitaine to his realm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Franks under Clovis I ended this expansion: in 507, allied with the Burgundians, they killed <b>Alaric II</b> at the Battle of Campus Vogladensis near Poitiers and sacked Toulouse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At Toulouse, <b>Alaric II</b>, great-grandson of Alaric I, who sacked Rome in 410, succeeded his father Euric as king of the Visigoths.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-28">28 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>With the permission of the Arian king <b>Alaric II</b>, Catholic bishops met at Agde and issued canons on clerical discipline, marriage and church property.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-10">10 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Syagrius fled to the Visigothic Kingdom, but <b>Alaric II</b> handed him over, and Clovis had him killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/domain-of-soissons">Domain of Soissons</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Visigothic Kingdom. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.58,0.34&amp;year=497" aria-label="Poitiers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Alaric II"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5020 6137h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5020 6137h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=46.58,0.34&amp;year=497">What was here in 497 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
